<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  design.md, "The Twister's names on Android": the firmware's USB descriptor
  gives manufacturer string "DJ TechTools" and product string "Midi Fighter
  Twister", with JackStrIndex = NO_DESCRIPTOR on every jack (no port names).
  A real USB MIDI 1.0 device gets those two strings concatenated into
  android.media.midi.MidiDeviceInfo's own PROPERTY_NAME by the framework's
  USB-MIDI registration path (MidiDeviceInfo.java's own PROPERTY_NAME
  javadoc: "For USB devices, this is a concatenation of the manufacturer
  and product names") - confirmed empirically too (a real Twister's ports
  reach JUCE as "DJ TechTools Midi Fighter Twister Output/Input Port 1",
  task 2.3's aliases, via juce_MidiDeviceListConnectionBroadcaster.cpp's
  `endpoint->getName() + " " + blockName`).

  A MidiDeviceService-declared virtual device never gets that PROPERTY_NAME
  concatenation: it is a USB-only framework behaviour, not something this
  file's schema (device manufacturer/product attributes, read by
  MidiService for PROPERTY_MANUFACTURER/PROPERTY_PRODUCT only) or the public
  MidiDeviceService API can set (confirmed by dumpsys media.midi on this
  device: its MidiDeviceInfo Bundle carries manufacturer/product but no name
  key at all). With no device-level name, JUCE's endpoint name is empty and
  the concatenation above degenerates to the bare per-port fallback
  ("Output Port 1"/"Input Port 1", juce_Midi_android.cpp's own
  `portName.isNotEmpty() ? portName : "..." + index`) - not what 2.3's
  aliases expect, and the F3 bug this file existed to catch.

  Fixed here at the one layer this virtual device CAN set: each port's own
  name (this schema's input-port/output-port name attribute, which JUCE
  reads as that same `portName` above). Naming the ports with the full
  manufacturer-prefixed string reaches the identical final JUCE name a real
  Twister produces, even though, unlike the real device, these two
  ports are not really nameless at the Android layer any more; there is no
  other public way for a virtual MidiDeviceService to present the same
  string a real USB device's PROPERTY_NAME concatenation produces.
-->
<devices>
    <device manufacturer="DJ TechTools" product="Midi Fighter Twister">
        <input-port name="DJ TechTools Midi Fighter Twister Input Port 1" />
        <output-port name="DJ TechTools Midi Fighter Twister Output Port 1" />
    </device>
</devices>
